'''''Gardenia''''' is a [[genus]] of [[flowering plant]]s in the coffee family, [[Rubiaceae]], native to the [[tropical]] and [[subtropical]] regions of [[Africa]], [[Asia]], [[Madagascar]], [[Pacific Islands]],<ref name=FOC>{{efloras|2|113259 |''Gardenia'' J. Ellis, ''Philos. Trans.'' '''51''': 935. 1761 |first1=Tao |last1=Chen |first2=Charlotte M. |last2=Taylor |volume=19}}</ref> and [[Australia]].<ref>{{cite journal|last=Puttock |first=C. F. |title=A revision of ''Gardenia'' Ellis (Rubiaceae) from north-eastern Queensland |journal=Austrobaileya |volume=2 |issue=5 |date=1988 |pages=433–449 |doi=10.5962/p.365726 |jstor=41738712|doi-access=free }}</ref>

The Gardenia genus was named by [[Carl Linnaeus]] (though the name was first proposed to him in 1753 by [[Jane Colden]], who often goes uncredited <ref>{{cite web |url=https://massivesci.com/articles/jane-colden-botany-colonial-america-new-york-marsh-st-johns-wort/ |title=Meet Jane Colden, the 18th century botanist snubbed by Linnaeus |author=Brittany Kenyon-Flatt |website=Massive Science |date=9 February 2021 |access-date=4 March 2026}}</ref>) and [[John Ellis (naturalist)|John Ellis]]. The genus was named after [[Alexander Garden (naturalist)|Alexander Garden]] (1730–1791), a [[Scotland|Scottish]] naturalist.<ref>{{Cite journal |doi = 10.1098/rstl.1759.0084|title = LXXXII. An account of the plants ''Halesia'' and ''Gardenia'': In a letter from John Ellis, Esq; F.R.S. To Philip Carteret Webb, Esq; F.R.S.|journal = Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London|volume = 51|pages = 929–935|year = 1759|s2cid = 186210416}}</ref> The type species is ''[[Gardenia jasminoides]]'', as first published by Ellis in 1761.<ref>Ellis J. ''Gardenia''; in Phil. Trans. Roy. Soc. 51(2): 935, t. 23 ('''1761''') nom. conserv.</ref>

==Description==
Gardenia species typically grow as shrubs or small trees, however some species, such as those native to New Guinea, may grow to {{Convert abbreviated|20-30|m}} tall.<ref name="FOC" /><ref name=":0">{{cite book |last=Rubiaceae |first=B. Verdcourt |title=Flora of Tropical East Africa |year=1976}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{cite book | last1=Utteridge | first1=Timothy M. A. | last2=Jennings | first2=Laura V. S. | title=Trees of New Guinea | publisher=Kew Publishing | date=2022 | isbn=978-1-84246-750-3|location= Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{cite web |author=M. Thulin |display-authors=etal |title=Flora of Somalia, Vol. 1-4 |date=2008 |url=https://plants.jstor.org/collection/FLOS}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{cite journal |title=Rubiaceae, part 3 |first1=D.M.|last1=Bridson |first2=B.|last2=Verdcourt |journal=Flora Zambesiaca |volume=5 |year=2003 |page=483}}</ref> A small number of species found in tropical East Africa and southern Africa grow as small [[Pyrophyte|pyrophytic]] [[subshrub]]s.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":3" /> At least one species, ''Gardenia epiphytica'', native to [[Gabon]] and [[Cameroon]], grows as an [[epiphyte]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Jongkind |first=Carel C. H. |year=2005 |title=Novitates Gabonenses 54. A New Species of Gardenia (Gardenieae, Rubiaceae) from Gabon |journal=Systematics and Geography of Plants |publisher=National Botanic Garden of Belgium |volume=75 |issue=2 |pages=259–262 |issn=1374-7886 |jstor=3668578}}</ref><ref>{{cite book | last1=Onana | first1=Jean-Michel | last2=Cheek | first2=Martin | title=Red Data Book of the Flowering Plants of Cameroon: IUCN Global Assessments | publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew | date=2011 | isbn=978-1-84246-429-8 |location= Kew, Richmond, Surrey, UK}}</ref><ref>{{cite iucn |author=Lovell, R. |author2=Cheek, M. |year=2021 |title=''Gardenia epiphytica'' |volume=2021 |article-number=e.T110093586A110093588 |doi=10.2305/IUCN.UK.2021-3.RLTS.T110093586A110093588.en |access-date=21 May 2025}}</ref> Most species are unarmed and spineless, but some species such as some of those found in Africa are spinescent.<ref name="FOC" /><ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" />

The leaf arrangement is typically opposite or verticillate may (arranged in whorls). Leaves vary by species; many species are glossy with a distinctly coriaceous (or leathery) texture such as that seen in ''[[Gardenia jasminoides]]'', whilst in others, leaves may be thin and chartaceous (or paper-like).<ref name="FOC" /><ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" />

The flowers, particularly in the species most commonly grown in gardens, may be large and showy and white, cream or pale yellow in color, with a pleasant and strong, sometimes overpowering scent that may be more noticeable at night, something quite typical of moth-pollinated plants.<ref name=":3" /><ref>{{Cite POWO|title=''Gardenia thunbergia'' Thunb. |id=751291-1 |access-date=1 October 2024}}</ref>

Gardenia flowers are [[Hermaphrodite (botany)|hermaphrodite]] (or bisexual) with each individual flower having both male and female structures (that is, having both [[stamen]]s and [[carpel]]s).<ref name="FOC" /> The [[flower|arrangement of the flower]]s on the plant are solitary or in small terminal clusters or fascicles. The flowers vary across species, but most commonly have a funnel- or cylindrical-shaped [[corolla (botany)|corolla]] tube, normally elongated and narrow distally, surrounded by 5–12 or more lobes ([[petal]]s) contorted or arranged in an overlapping pattern.<ref name="FOC" /><ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" />

==Phytochemistry==
[[Crocetin]] is a chemical compound usually obtained from ''[[Crocus sativus]]'', which can also be obtained from the fruit of ''Gardenia jasminoides''.{{R|CROCETIN}} Gordonin is a novel methoxylated flavonol secreted in golden-colored resinous droplets of ''Gardenia gordonii''<ref>{{cite journal |author1=John M. Miller |author2=Subramaniam Sotheeswaran |date=March 1993 |title=Bud Exudate Composition and Ecogeography of Fijian Gardenia Species (Rubiaceae) |journal=Biotropica |volume=25 |issue=1 |pages=117–122 |doi=10.2307/2388981}}</ref>, which is one of several critically endangered species of the Fiji Islands.

Many of the native gardenias of the Pacific Islands and elsewhere in the [[paleotropics]] contribute towards the production of a diverse array of natural products. [[Methoxy group|Methoxylated]] and [[oxygenate]]d [[flavonols]], [[flavones]], and [[triterpene]]s accumulate on the vegetative and floral buds as yellow to brown droplets of secreted resins. Many focused [[Phytochemistry|phytochemical]] studies of these bud exudates have been published, including a population-level study of two rare, [[Sympatry|sympatric]] species of [[Fiji]], ''[[Gardenia candida|G.&nbsp;candida]]'' and ''[[Gardenia grievei|G.&nbsp;grievei]]''.{{R|MILLER}} The evolutionary significance of the [[Natural gum|gums]] and resins of gardenias in attracting or repelling [[invertebrate]] [[herbivore]]s was explored by ecologists in 2018; specifically in the context of the repellent, insecticidal, and anti-oviposition activities of the [[ethanol]]-extracted<ref>{{cite web |url=https://extraktlab.com/ethanol-extraction/ |title=Ethanol Extraction: Explained |author=extraktLAB |access-date=4 March 2026}} |{{date missing}}</ref> essential oil of [[Gardenia]] jasminoides against [[whiteflies]] and [[mites]]<ref>{{cite web |url=https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6010415/ |title=Repellency, toxicity, and anti-oviposition of essential oil of Gardenia jasminoides and its four major chemical components against whiteflies and mites |author1=Tufail Ahmed Wagan |author2=Wanlun Cai |author3=Hongxia Hua |website=National Library Of Medicine |date=20 June 2018 |access-date=4 March 2026}}</ref>.

